  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nIn the wake of widespread thunderstorm activity this morning,\nheat has once again been tempered over much of the area with\nsome late afternoon recovery expected to bring temperatures back\nup to the low 90s by afternoon in a corridor from Jacksonville\nto Robinson, with heat index likely to reach around 105 south of\nI-70 as a warm front lifts northward through the area. Mid to\nupper 80s can be expected to the north. \n\nA few showers remain in this frontal zone, where perhaps a few\nthunderstorms could develop this afternoon as diurnal \ndestabilization gradually takes place.\n\nTonight, a cold front will approach from the northwest, with\nwarm advection ahead of the front promoting another cluster or\ntwo of thunderstorms. Areas of significant precipitation are\npossible, although much less likely than last night. The\nlocation of any heavy precipitation swaths remains too uncertain\nfor a Flood Watch at this point, but we will continue to\nevaluate any corridors for potential Watch issuance. If expected\nheavy precipitation corridors line up well with last night's\nheavy precipitation from Fulton to Vermilion County these would\nbe favorable for renewed flooding. 12Z high-res average model \nguidance points to a 1.5 to 1.5 inch corridor north of Peoria \nand Bloomington, although there remain considerable run-to-run \nand model-to-model discrepancies. SPC continues a Marginal Risk \nfor locally damaging winds with thunderstorms both tonight and \nSunday as these storms move across central IL and some afternoon\nthunderstorms as diurnal destabilization takes place and the \ncold front lingers over the southeast half of central IL.\n\nA couple of dry days are forecast Monday and Tuesday behind\nSunday's front, then another cold front midweek will bring the\nnext chance for thunderstorms.\n\nTemperatures will remain quite warm and humid for central IL as\n2 inch precipitable water values keep dewpoints well into the\n70s and highs reach the 80s, to lower 90s south of I-70. A Heat\nAdvisory continues for areas south of I-70 until Sunday evening\nas heat index values will near 105 each afternoon until then.\nHighs will be in the 80s across the area Monday through\nTuesday, then the midweek front will continue to lower\ntemperatures for late in the week, with some upper 70s possible.",
